\>>Trajectories

There's a distinction between trajectory and physical continuity (because force isn't transmitted along trajectories; it can be if one proposes a continuous object in time). But there's no difference between the abstract and the "real". Because we do not have access to the "real". We only have observations, and the abstractions we construct with reference to those observations. Neither the observation nor the model gives us access to what "actually" is.&#x20;

Also... you need to get beyond the "is this or is this not a connection to 4D thinking". That's established. And the intellectual community that evolved from what started in the 4D era is well beyond the 4th dimension... it's one, two, three, many dimensions. And your "one community's popularly discredited conceptual world adopted as another field's mathematically codified understandings is still a question" ...is off base. Sure, there are plenty of cases of independent discovery and indirect or direct transfer with or without continuity of all of the specifics (in fact, I'm persuaded to believe that String Theory was an independent development that was later recognized as, and unified with Braid Theory by Witten). And yes, it's smart to consider specific individual-level network evidence... but importantly, that is *not* the analytical level that should condition our credulity. In a population of billions, that's what you need to establish to demonstrate community and network. But even the entirety of the scientific community and educated lay audience is not a population of billions. Narrow that to mathematical physicists and experimental physicists [and whatever other general field divides], as broadly constituted in the late 19th, early 20th century (which was obviously smaller than it is today, and it's still small today, especially if you narrow that to publishing academics, which, overlain on the institutional and geographic distribution, is \*by definition\* the interconnectedness level where science actually happens, where concepts actually are communicated). So--if we want to understand the movement of ideas, we have to analyze on the level of a community of that size with its particular shape and internal dynamics. And crucially: *We cannot analyze ideas as if they lived in papers*. They do not; they live in humans. Humans who can have fifty year scientific careers. In a highly interconnected community that would probably fit in a large convention center. We've already seen just the bare traces (I've given you a handful of the hundreds of German language sources I found) of the *public* footprint of "four-dimensionalism" in the late 19th-early 20th centuries. The activities of a tiny intellectual community alone rarely make a splash in the papers, let alone create the wide cultural wake that's already obvious in the first trickle of sources. Just by population differential, it's clear that *popular* conceptions of the fourth dimension, even if it had a fraction of the following as it appears to have had in the sciences, it would have to be orders of magnitude larger than the scientific community... or even the community of spiritualist thought-leaders. That context matters, and it's evident in the articles we've seen. You don't see front page articles in the Columbus Dispatch or the New York Post on even such popular topics as black holes or quantum computers very often. But the germanophone information environment--and I suspect at least the British, and probably (perhaps less so, perhaps not), the American press--will show some similar disparity between the coverage we'd expect of typical physics, and the historical coverage of the 4th dimension. I suspect also, that part of our present unfamiliarity, and hence experience of rediscovery, here is likely a combination of the US/UK divide, and the British/Continental divide, and especially the English/German divide in the scientific community at the time. Perhaps the popular narrative in Germany is different -- but then again, they have their own, very different but directly related post-WWII amnesia. So who knows.&#x20;

Also--we're not considering the question of what science is "correct" or not, so get that out of your vocabulary. We are not worrying about whether implicature of monotonicity is tantamount to assertion of physical continuity, we are not classifying and judging the mathematical criteria for scientific provenance and attribution. What we are considering is a sociology of science and an Acadamia-Publicum interface question. The purely historical vs physically consequential extension of the worldtube is potentially a significant dividing line. What terms and formalisms are used is, potentially in part a linguistic badging question, and probably in part a community continuity question, but it is not, for us, an especially consequential matter in regard to its field-specific particularities and divergences. We can trace the nLab lineages later, if we want to get into the weeds on documenting and sorting out the provenance of the ideas that SAT converged upon/reinvented/knowingly and unknowingly absorbed. And the same is largely true of our investigation of the international academic community and its geographic, linguistic, and professional faultlines and continuities... as a *professional cultural* and sociological question. The structure of the literature (academic and popular) will inform, and sometimes have direct impacts on the questions we're asking and directions we turn our attention, but the scientific attribution and crediting is a separate and distinct matter that, when and if we get to that, we'll tackle separately; but I already have a separate work lane for that. &#x20;

So... if that all makes sense... go ahead and read as your interest takes you, and see what kind of opinions you form--careful not to import unwarranted hyperskepticism any more than unwarranted paradoeilia. Just look at the particulars of the nLab community. And look at what they talk about and how they talk about it in their less technical registers--the site and article level introductions, the inline asides and quips, the personality and flavor.

And *then* read nPOV, and *then* read the forums, the personal pages, the n-Category Cafe material. And along the way, not in lieu of, but auxiliary to your general and specific impressions of the intellectual and personal character of the material, note the distinctions that the modifier "higher" is used to denote.&#x20;

- SAT's filaments are physical-theoretical structures whose interactions with the time surface are intended to account for particle properties and dynamics.



No. SAT has no ontology, and asserts that no ontology is knowable. The only thing that can be coherently described as "knowable" is observation. Models organize observed patterns. That's the whole of it. If a model can be constructed captures the observed behaviors and also leverages human physical and geometric intuitions, then that is a preference, and that preference is baked into SAT's methodology. But SAT/H(s)H makes no ontological or even, for that matter, physical claims unless we want to get into what ontological continuity within perceived observation constitutes sufficient predictability to label something "real" or "physical". But none of that is necessary to do the work; we broadly adopt the conceptual conventions of science and everyday thinking (and my own quirky idiolect) out of linguistic convenience because it doesn't matter whether one thinks of measurements as a subset of perceptions, or if one has the metaphysical conviction that there is something "more" to which perceptions adhere--that boils down to a label, and asserting something as "actually" real or "merely" abstract only has meaning within particular convictions about what those terms *ought* to mean. So, whatever. The biggest intellectual step of SAT--and until I discovered Hinton, I thought I was the first one *since* Hinton (or rather, since I wasn't aware of him, I thought I was the *only* one apart from, I'd presumed, maybe some science fiction writers or obscure mathematicians) is simply the choice of asking questions about--and finding possible answers--regarding whether treating physical continuity, resistance to being bent, etc is a productive line of inquiry (and, as is immediately obvious from the Minkowski worldline/worldtube diagram of an accelerating mass, yes--3D acceleration is mathematically identical to 4D tensile strength, whether or not you suppose that is the only quality in which worldtubes can be equated to physical objects).&#x20;



So... you still haven't seen the point number four that I'm nudging you towards. Consider, in particular, the *tone* of and *register* evident in the less technical facets of the encyclopedic pages, and consider the chosen quotations and how they're used. But mostly the tone and register. It's rather distinct.&#x20;

"Higher" is intended to sound technical, and it is... but it's an avoidance "higher category theory" is just the name of the field, omitting the distasteful d-word. Most everywhere else in science, it's just a label. But this community is careful to use "higher" for its original intended purpose: to identify a field as addressing higher dimensions, or not. The d-word is no longer truly taboo (if that's the right word for what it was, but it *was* at some point clearly *dropped*)... the use of "higher" has a phantom limb from an era when that limb was considered too unseemly to saddle oneself with. Put your linguist hat on. This is what happens when a community is subject to existential external and internal threat, and those threats have teeth. It's the same reason dice became dominoes and dominoes became playing cards and playing cards became floral hanafuta cards. When you can't make--or talk about--the things you're interested in, you find other ways to communicate. We can't talk about dimensions (or we couldn't for a good long time), but we can talk about the higher-dimensional *intersections* and *connections* and *structural relationships* in the abstract... as... categories. Very neutral. Groupoids... impenetrable, even better.&#x20;

But again, you're drifting too deeply into the mathematical and conceptual weeds. I'm talking about a *tone*. I'm asking you to consider the *use of* *language*, independently of the subjects being discussed.&#x20;

\>>Nor would I treat the nLab as a miniature version of the late nineteenth-century four-dimensional milieu.

No, it's one--probably one of many--direct offspring of a previously aggressively fragmented and retreated intellectual community. As in any community, there are countervailing trends and there is diversity, and as in any community that has been subject to deeply traumatic existential threat, it bears those scars linguistically, attitudinally, behaviorally, and socially. A century ago, 4D thinkers were poised to be the absolute pillars of the next century of physics. Instead, they got branded, beaten down, and driven out. But the sheer newspaper footprint--and magnitude of the scientific legacy of the field and some of its heavyweights--shows you that it wasn't some tiny obscure niche, as it's come to be imagined. Although, maybe it has atrophied to the degree that it is one or several or many quietly productive niches. That *together* has continued to be the unsung workhorse of modern physics. *That* is the scientific legacy here. Whether nLab is the only community that is aware of the continuity of that legacy is uncertain, but seems unlikely. The number of alternate models of physics that *maintain mainstream credibility* coming out of knot theory and braid theory and its big bushy family of--well, it's the bushes that grew from the sawed-off trunk--is what tells that story. And let me be clear: It appears, even in the limited look we've gotten--that that trunk was either one of two major pillars of pre-Einsteinian physics, or it was the establishment of that physics. Or... and I think this is likely, and likely continues to be the case: The nature of higher dimensionality as one way of describing time, is largely taken for granted and not thought too hard about in physics broadly; hell, anyone who's ever drawn a trajectory is doing exactly that. But, within some surviving specialist communities, the conviction remains that *this fact is much more important and consequential than is recognized*. And that's true. And, as I said, four dimensions was a door, not a limitation. Recognizing time (or some other construction) as a dimension immediately presents one with questions of combinatorics, corespondences, indirect--or direct but unobserved--relationships, and so on... the easiest way of tackling that combinatoric space, and the first approach of humanity to anything complex, is to categorize and find relationships. The 20th century early knot and braid theorists did exactly that--recognized the significance of it, and made categorization a discipline in its own right. And braid theorists found a way to talk about worldlines that behaved exactly as worldlines do by setting up a couple of hard rules that physical rope and string braids do not have to abide by. Monotonicity has *no* place in a theory about the interweaving of threadlike objects. It serves one purpose: To impose a precise isomorphism to the condition of *time* onto the otherwise ropelike worldlines. That's not an arbitrary choice. It was a necessity. And--as fond as scientists are of vocabulary--you don't *just* change the name of a basic notion, lie *time* for funsies. You do it to continue your research when--especially in a field that had just *recently* come into its own at the time of the Victorian 4D craze--when your institution, and perhaps some of your colleagues, and you yourself find it impossible to distance yourself from a *different* community, of whom you may or may not approve, but a community to which *some* of your colleagues maintain active connections.... and sometimes get driven out of the scientific community for it.&#x20;

But you're still missing the tone. There's a sort of... reverence. A sort of ...talking *around* things. [Actually the entire field appears to be an exercise in absolute linguistic impeneterability (coming from someone who *gets off on* impenetrable vernacular), and nLab is a tour de force] ...on the one hand, that's exactly the point I'm making above. It's basic sociolinguistics. But on the other hand, there's something particular about the *register* and *style* and *tone* in which the material--especially the openings of articles, especially in the overarching mission statement type material, but also somewhat in some of the articles' opening ledes and parentheticals.&#x20;

The nLab \*is\* a direct descendent of the 4D milieux. *Plural*. 4D thinking was widespread. In physics and in the public imagination. And no, you don't collapse fields into one another--you properly categorize the overlapping intellectual descendents of the 4D ...of physics. The descendants of what physics was, or was shaping up to be. And its historically deliberate avoidance of exactly the sort of language that got it branded in the first place underscores the self-recognition point. Do not confuse coded language for unrelated conceptual territory. *That's the point*.&#x20;

What I would posit is that, whether or not any given researcher has any particular belief or ontological commitment, for a field whose connections to spiritualism was... apparently, nothing short of catastrophic. And the historical material has given us a *glimpse* of the enormity of that era, and a sense of the almost apocalyptic existential ...not even threat. The teeth were bared, and used to devour physicists. Such that, it would seem, it was sufficient to make Einstein go from essentially "Yeah, people don't get 4D" ...to "4D? You must be thinking of someone else". And it's *important* that the community survived and continued to be, not just productive, but *the foundation of physics* to this day. Even if they've (intentionally at first) abstractified into something that looks entirely different, even if most physicists today, and perhaps most braid theorists even, don't think of the work in oncrete *ropelike* terms... that's where it came from. All of it. And most of modern physics apart from, arguably *some* quantum theory. You can't just erase a field whose practitioners were the giants and the workaday academics; not without banishing half of mathematics to the occult. Whose work is *actually* and *functionally* continuously productive. But what you can do is rebrand. Abstractify. Recognize in some sense the same thing that SAT recognizes: Doesn't matter whether you call it abstract or concrete; your model is abstract either way (and, though SAT is unusually specific on this point), your conceptualization and perception of it is just as abstract. So--just go whole-hog abstract. Why not? If I can draw a sphere on a 2D piece of paper... I can represent any concept in any medium. If you set it up right, it'll still do the work. And it has. Physics is *still* undergirded by a, much-transformed but not everywhere forgotten, *foundational* understanding of the temporal topography of particles. And the entirety of SM, much of GM, and QCD is still an outgrowth of the Hinton/Kelvin school. And that's why from Einstein-Cartan to Kaluza-Klein to (retroactively) String Theory and M-Theory... and Bohmian mechanics, and I don't know how many others, but the nLab group can *and does* name every one of them, meticulously.&#x20;

Four-Dimensionalism didn't go underground--*mathemtical physics* went underground. And, over a couple of generations, widely forgot that that's what happened.&#x20;

carried out, and self-imposed.

